1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,535
The Pythagorean theorem says that, in a right triangle,

2
00:00:03,535 --> 00:00:08,548
the squares of the two shorter sides add up to the square of the longest side.

3
00:00:09,166 --> 00:00:13,727
Here is a right triangle. The two shorter sides, a and b,

4
00:00:13,727 --> 00:00:18,527
are called the legs. The longest side, c, is the hypotenuse.

5
00:00:20,133 --> 00:00:23,289
To prove it, take four copies of this triangle,

6
00:00:23,289 --> 00:00:27,185
and place them inside a big square whose side is a plus b.

7
00:00:27,800 --> 00:00:31,216
The triangles leave a tilted square in the middle,

8
00:00:31,216 --> 00:00:35,315
with side c. So the big square's area is the four triangles,

9
00:00:35,315 --> 00:00:36,340
plus c squared.

10
00:00:36,966 --> 00:00:39,746
Now slide the triangles to new spots.

11
00:00:39,746 --> 00:00:42,075
The total area does not change.

12
00:00:42,699 --> 00:00:46,144
This time, the leftover space is two squares:

13
00:00:46,144 --> 00:00:48,976
one with side a, and one with side b.

14
00:00:49,599 --> 00:00:52,530
Same big square, same four triangles.

15
00:00:52,530 --> 00:00:56,411
So c squared must equal a squared plus b squared.

16
00:00:56,411 --> 00:00:58,945
That is the Pythagorean theorem.
